    "sun": "ordinary volume is where the six actually live, and it gets taken, correctly enough, for one reasonable person. a night at full amplitude is the single occasion on which anyone has watched the count change in front of them — and a change witnessed under those conditions gets filed as damage rather than as information. so the plural is on record, everywhere, entered only in the worst hours.",
    "moon": "full volume gets read everywhere as the truth arriving — the manners off, the real one finally out. here that reading fails in a specific way: the loud one holds no rank over the other five, and never has. what it does simply will not come off afterwards, which is why it is the only one of you that has ever left evidence.",
    "pull": "the one they call the real you is whichever of you was loudest. it has no better claim on your name than the five who have never raised their voices.",
    "tension": "people who have known you for years, meaning nothing cruel by it, arrive at volatile — a word about weather, applied to somebody who supposedly has one. the account that would actually fit — that the shouting one and the patient one are both members, neither senior, neither a mask over the other — cannot be said in the week after a bad night. it arrives as a person explaining that whoever did it was somebody else.",
    "cost": "you can go all the way to the bottom of a night and be working by the second morning, because what went down was one of six and the rest kept the week running. that recovery is the strongest thing anyone holds against you. a person who is fine on tuesday is understood to have been fine about it, and nothing felt afterwards outweighs the speed of the return."
