    "moon": "room does not run on belief. what you hold open for him costs nothing to keep open, asks no question about whether it is warranted, and can be extended indefinitely without anybody deciding to extend it. so the space goes on being real — genuinely his, genuinely unclaimed — while the finding goes on being accurate, and the two have never had occasion to meet.",
    "pull": "nine years of a place kept for a man you finished reading in the first month, and from where he is sitting, patience and hope are the same object.",
    "tension": "people say you never gave up on him, kindly, in front of him, about a man everyone else wrote off years ago. giving up was never on the table — you read him once, correctly, and set the place anyway, which has no name anybody in that room would recognise. the correction is unavailable: saying you stopped expecting him in the first autumn turns nine years of loyalty into nine years of something colder, and that is the honest version.",
    "cost": "nobody has to earn a way back to you — no deadline gets set, no ultimatum, no year in which the offer quietly lapses, and that is rarer than people think. the room carries no evidence of expectation, since expectation was never what kept it open. so what he has had for nine years is unlimited space from somebody who settled his case before that year was out."
